+### 14.1X Android AI-Guided Mobile QA/UX/UI Testing Unit — Current State in this v3.1 change set
+
+**Purpose:** establish a repeatable first-round Android testing system that behaves like a user across Rally26's real personas, fields, navigation, WebViews, authority tiers, and subscription states; captures visible regressions and UX/UI defects; and produces evidence that can be triaged into launch-blocking bugs. This unit complements, but never replaces, backend/service/integration/security testing.
+
+**Selected tool:** Firebase App Distribution **App Testing agent (Android)**, currently a preview Gemini-powered testing feature. Test cases are written in natural-language YAML, versioned with the repository, and executed against an Android APK through the Firebase CLI. Rally26's registered Android package must match the real app package `com.rally26.mobile`. No Firebase SDK is required solely to register/distribute/test the app through this feature; adding unrelated Firebase runtime SDKs is not part of this decision.
+
+**Repository contract (`qa/`):**
+- `qa/firebase/android/tests/` — suite-per-persona/state YAML, with `[SMOKE]` cases for the fast first pass and fuller regression cases for deeper review;
+- `qa/firebase/android/run-suite.sh` — one-suite CLI wrapper using `firebase apptesting:execute`, a Firebase App ID, device list, and runtime-only credential files;
+- `qa/firebase/android/.credentials/` — local/CI secret files only, ignored by Git; passwords never belong in YAML or source control;
+- `qa/matrices/ROLE-FEATURE-MATRIX.md` — expected feature visibility/authority by `OWNER`, `ADMINISTRATOR`, `VIEWER`, `TEAM_MANAGER`, `TEAM_EDITOR`, `COACH_READ`, Parent/Guardian, Athlete, and Platform Admin;
+- `qa/matrices/SUBSCRIPTION-GATING-MATRIX.md` — owner onboarding/subscription lifecycle plus plan-entitlement expectations;
+- `qa/matrices/COVERAGE-MATRIX.md` — maps product areas to executable suites;
+- `qa/test-data/TEST-ACCOUNTS.md` — stable QA organization/account/data-state requirements;
+- `qa/triage/` — P0-P3 severity and reproducible bug-report format using Firebase screenshots/video/action trace/accessibility view/logs.
+
+**Initial Android suite contract:** cover at least these distinct test identities/states rather than one over-permissioned account:
+1. Coach with `TEAM_MANAGER`, plus separate `TEAM_EDITOR` and `COACH_READ` authority accounts.
+2. Parent/Guardian linked to a representative multi-athlete/multi-team household.
+3. Athlete with a real `ATHLETE_SELF` link; athlete tests must verify self/schedule/guardian/eligibility privacy and SafeSport-scoped messaging without financial or teammate-roster leakage.
+4. Active Owner, separate Organization `ADMINISTRATOR`, and Organization `VIEWER`.
+5. Platform Admin, which currently validates the honest mobile boundary rather than forcing a customer persona.
+6. Disposable/resettable incomplete Owner for organization → plan → review → Stripe checkout/cancel/resume.
+7. Separate subscription-state owners for no-organization, PLAN, REVIEW, CHECKOUT_PENDING, ACTIVE, PAST_DUE, and CANCELED/SUSPENDED behavior.
+8. Separate Starter and Club organizations for visible plan-entitlement behavior. Starter is expected to enforce the current backend limits (three-team cap and plan-gated SMS/SportsEngine/TeamSnap/QuickBooks); the AI suite validates the user-visible upgrade/availability behavior while backend tests prove the restrictions cannot be bypassed directly.
+
+**Required QA behavior:**
+- Assertions describe what is visibly true on the final screen; do not ask the agent to infer invisible database/webhook state.
+- Every major role sweep checks normal success paths plus loading/empty/error/unauthorized presentation, field visibility/usability, scrolling, keyboard/safe-area behavior, clipped/overlapping content, dead taps, and misleading controls.
+- Authority tests verify both positive access and negative leakage. Hiding/disabling a control is UX only; the backend remains the security boundary and must keep direct-API authorization tests.
+- Owner onboarding tests explicitly prove that a saved `DRAFT` organization, selected plan, checkout return, or pending webhook cannot unlock native Owner routes. Only the authoritative server state documented in §14.1R/`isOwnerAccessUnlocked` may unlock.
+- Financial, payout, refund, transfer, destructive member, live checkout, and other irreversible actions stay non-destructive in the normal AI suite unless a dedicated disposable/test-mode workflow is designed for that mutation.
+- WebView tests verify authenticated mobile-width usability and correct persona destination, not just that a URL loaded.
+- Cross-organization/household/youth-data leakage is P0.
+- The same YAML suite may be run on more than one Android model/version, but the first baseline should use one representative phone to conserve preview quota and reduce noisy variance; broaden device coverage only after smoke tests are stable.
+- Firebase's AI test result is evidence, not truth: review screenshots, action trace, accessibility view, video, and logs for failures and suspicious passes.
+
+**Execution order for the first AI QA round:** shared first-launch/login → Coach → Parent → Athlete → Owner → Platform Admin boundary → Owner onboarding → authority tiers → subscription lifecycle/entitlements. Run `[SMOKE]` first and close P0/P1 findings before spending quota on full regression/device expansion.
+
+**Maintenance rule:** any mobile change that alters a route, visible field, action, authority boundary, onboarding/subscription gate, or WebView destination must update the affected `qa/firebase/android` YAML and the relevant matrix in the same change. A new backend capability/role/plan entitlement that changes mobile behavior is incomplete until its positive and negative Android QA expectations are represented.
+
+**Current limitation / iOS boundary:** Firebase App Testing agent in this decision is Android-specific. It is not evidence of iOS correctness. Once Android reaches the founder-approved stable baseline, choose and document the iOS equivalent (TestFlight/device UAT plus an automation framework/provider appropriate to the then-current Expo/iOS toolchain) in a separate ADR rather than forcing Android's Firebase implementation onto iOS.
+
+**Acceptance criteria:**
+1. The `qa/` unit is committed with parseable Firebase YAML and no credentials.
+2. Firebase registers `com.rally26.mobile` exactly and the project has an App ID available to local/CI execution.
+3. Dedicated QA accounts/data states exist for each primary persona, authority tier, and subscription state used by a suite.
+4. All `[SMOKE]` suites execute against the same release-candidate APK on at least one representative Android phone.
+5. P0/P1 failures are fixed or explicitly accepted with owner, reason, mitigation, and rollback before pilot release.
+6. Permission/subscription failures found by the agent are backed by direct backend regression tests when the defect could be bypassed outside the UI.
+7. Full regression is rerun after blocker fixes; screenshots/videos/traces are retained long enough to support triage.
+8. Android success is recorded separately from iOS readiness.
+
+**Reference:** `docs/adr/ADR-120-android-ai-guided-mobile-qa.md` and Firebase's current official App Testing agent documentation. Re-verify preview status, CLI flags, devices, quotas, and limits before changing CI/release policy because this provider surface is explicitly preview software.
+
